The '''Communist Party''' ({{lang-bl|Partido Comunista}}; '''PC''') was a [[W:Communist party|Marxist political party]] in [[Balisca]], initially founded in 1884 by leftists dissatisfied with the [[1883 Confederal Convention]], who supported [[W:State socialism|state socialism]] ("[[W:Socialism from above|socialism from above]]") as a way to guide the transition a [[W:Communist society|communist society]]. The party would ultimately ally with [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchists]] and more moderate socialists in a [[W:Popular front|popular front]] against the government of [[Máximo Burjassot]], and was instrumental in coordinating direct action during the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. The movement during this time would find itself infiltrated by the [[W:Communist International|Communist International]] (Comintern), which led to the formation of a [[W:Leninism|Leninist]] faction which sought to establish itself as a [[W:Vanguard party|vanguard party]]. 

This faction came to dominate the party by the eve of [[1919 Confederal Convention]], and it's deepening ties to the increasingly authoritarian [[W:Bolsheviks|Bolsheviks]] led to increased friction with predominately anarchist-aligned [[Trade unions in Balisca|trade unions]] and [[W:Soviet (council)|soviets]], which worked to undermine the party. Continuing internal ideological divisions weakened the party's influence and appeal during elections for the convention's delegates, allowing anarchists to sweep a majority of both urban and rural delegations. Seeking to prevent the consolidation of anarchist influence over the post-revolution reforms, armed communist militias [[1919 Baliscan coup attempt|attempted to forcibly dissolve]] the convention in [[Castejón]] and organize a [[W:Dual power|parallel government]]. 

Without widespread support from the [[W:Working class|working class]], and plagued by infighting, these uprisings were quickly and violently put down after [[1919 Baliscan coup attempt|several days]] of fighting by the [[Autonomous Militia|Autonomous Militias]]. Rebels would eventually lay down their arms and were spared from execution by the communes, which sought criminal trials only against the leaders of the movement and collaborators. 

Widely discredited by the failed uprising, the Communist Party ultimately disbanded following the enactment of the [[Basic Law: Political Parties, Organization and Representation|Article IV]] of the [[Basic Laws of Balisca|Basic Laws]].

